routes.brief:
First prerequisite: Hochschild, cyclotomic and refined trace methods (RefinedTraceMethods). RT.2 plans cyclotomic spectra, TC, TC^−, TP and the Nikolaus–Scholze formula. RT.3 plans the cyclotomic trace and the Dundas–Goodwillie–McCarthy theorem, and asks for 'the map-level square for … a henselian pair in the proven range' without planning a proof. This Part II supplies that theorem with its proof and its consequences. RT.3's henselian export, and the Part II proposed by the AMMN extraction (RefinedTraceMethodsPartIIPadicDeformationOfKTheoryClasses), should import Theorem 4.36 from here.

Final theorems, as the paper states them (arXiv v2 numbering):
- Theorem 4.36 (Theorem A). For a henselian pair (R, I) and every prime p, K^inv(R) → K^inv(R/I) is an equivalence modulo p; equivalently K(R, I)/p ≃ TC(R, I)/p.
- Theorem 2.7 and Corollary 2.15 (Theorem G). TC/p commutes with all colimits on CycSp_{≥0}, and with sifted colimits of connective E_1-rings.
- Theorem 6.5 (Theorem B). If R is henselian along (p), R/p has finite Krull dimension and [k(x):k(x)^p] ≤ p^d with d ≥ 1, then K(R)/p^r → TC(R)/p^r is an equivalence in degrees ≥ d. Corollary 6.6 is the p-adic Lichtenbaum–Quillen statement.
- Theorem 6.1 (Theorem C) and Theorem 6.3. K^inv(R)/p = 0 for R strictly henselian local of residue characteristic p, and K̂^ét(X)/p ≃ TC(X)/p for X proper over a ring henselian along (p).
- Theorem 6.11 (Theorem D). Split injectivity for local F_p-algebras, with the complement identified in Proposition 6.12.
- Theorem 5.5 (Theorem F). K(R) → lim K(R/I^s) is an equivalence mod p for R noetherian, I-adically complete, with R/p F-finite. Theorem 5.7 shows the F-finiteness hypothesis is needed.
- Theorems 5.26 and 5.31 (Theorem E). Pro Geisser–Levine for F-finite regular local F_p-algebras, with Corollaries 5.33–5.35.

Cover every construction on the way, in this order.
(1) Finiteness of TC/p (§2):
- connectivity (Lemma 2.5);
- trivial cyclotomic spectra and HF_p^triv, with Lemma 2.10 (Frobenius kills x);
- the cofibre sequences of Lemma 2.11 and TC(X) ≃ fib(X → X_{hS^1}) for HF_p^triv-modules (Proposition 2.12);
- the approximation of Proposition 2.19 and the pro-constancy of TC^r/p (Lemmas 2.20–2.21, Proposition 2.22);
- the de Rham–Witt route for F_p-algebras (Proposition 2.26), and Proposition 2.27 for strict Dieudonné complexes, which the paper leaves to the reader: supply its proof.
(2) Nonunital henselian rings (§3):
- nonunital, local and henselian nonunital algebras, their reflections and free objects, and the Lawvere-theory point of view;
- Gabber's criterion that henselianity depends only on I (Proposition 3.16);
- henselization and Milnor squares (Lemma 3.21, Corollary 3.22), and free henselian Q-algebras as colimits (Corollary 3.23).
(3) Pseudocoherence (§4.1):
- perfect and pseudocoherent functors, relative to a subcategory;
- their characterizations and closure properties;
- the Goodwillie tower of Σ^∞Ω^∞ and the passage through Σ^∞Ω^∞ and HZ.
(4) The axiomatic rigidity argument: Lemma 4.15 and Proposition 4.16, with the simplicial-resolution description (Corollary 4.18).
(5) Projective pseudocoherence of C_*(BGL_∞(Z ⋉ I)), K(Z ⋉ I), HI, THH and TC/p (Propositions 4.19–4.26), using van der Kallen's homological stability, which nothing else plans.
(6) The equal-characteristic case:
- ν^n and ν̃^n, and Lemma 4.28;
- the Geisser–Levine and Geisser–Hesselholt theorems, stated as imported theorems with their exact hypotheses;
- Proposition 4.31 (rigidity of ν̃) and Proposition 4.32.
(7) The proof of Theorem 4.36 via K^inv of nonunital rings and Proposition 4.34 / Corollary 4.35. Record Remark 4.39's integral counterexample.
(8) I-adic continuity: Lemmas 5.2–5.3, Proposition 5.4 with Dundas–Morrow, Remark 5.6, Theorem 5.7 with Lemma 5.8 and the Bloch–Esnault–Kerz bound (correcting the index slips of sourceIssues E3).
(9) Pro Geisser–Levine:
- Morrow's logarithmic Hodge–Witt results;
- Proposition 5.27, Lemmas 5.29–5.30 and the pro HKR theorem of Dundas–Morrow;
- Theorem 5.31 and Corollaries 5.32–5.35.
(10) §6:
- Theorem 6.1, Proposition 6.2 and the étale sheaf reformulation;
- Theorem 6.3, with Clausen–Mathew's hyperdescent inputs and Lemma 6.4;
- Theorem 6.5, Corollary 6.6 and Examples 6.7–6.10;
- Theorem 6.11 and Proposition 6.12.

Import, never re-plan:
- RefinedTraceMethods RT.2 (cyclotomic spectra, the Nikolaus–Scholze formula, classical TR/TC, THH of rings, and Hesselholt's HKR theorem routed there by this extraction) and RT.3 (the trace, Dundas–Goodwillie–McCarthy, K^inv excision).
- GeneralAlgebraicKTheory K.2, K.5, K.6, K.7 (K-theory of rings and the plus construction, relative and nonconnective K-theory, Bass excision, filtered colimits) and StableHomotopyKTheory H.3, H.6 (plus construction, E/m, Bockstein and Milnor sequences).
- KTheoryFiniteLocalFields L.2 (Gabber rigidity away from p).
- SchemeKTheoryOperations S.4 (Thomason–Trobaugh descent) and S.6 (Adams operations).
- CrystallineCohomology CR.4 (de Rham–Witt complexes, strict Dieudonné complexes, logarithmic forms and Illusie's sequence) and DerivedDeRhamCohomology DD.3 (Cartier isomorphism).
- HigherLocalFieldsAndHigherClassFieldTheory HL.2 (logarithmic de Rham–Witt coefficients).
- EnhancedDerivedSheaves E3 (Kan extensions).
- ArithmeticLocallySymmetricSpaces ALS.2 (Borel–Serre).
- SchemeAndStackFoundations SF.0 and SF.2 (henselian pairs, henselization, Elkik, Popescu, étale cohomology and proper base change) and Tau Ceti's Modular Curves layer 4D (strict henselization).
- DeformationAndDerivedPatchingAlgebra R03.1/R03.3 (F-finiteness and excellence).
- ArcTopologyAndDescent (Gabber's affine proper base change).
- Mathlib's HenselianRing and HenselianLocalRing.
The p-adic continuity theorems of §5.2 go to the AMMN Part II, which this roadmap supplies with Theorem 4.36.

Unit tests:
- a square-zero extension (Dundas–Goodwillie–McCarthy);
- (Z_p, (p)) and O_{C_p} (Example 6.10);
- F_p[t]^h at (t) against F_p;
- the perfection of F_p[[x]] for Remark 4.39;
- k[[t]] with k = F_p(t_1, t_2, …) for Theorem 5.7;
- F_p[x]/(x^2) for Theorem D.
Supply a suggested Lean file.

This route adds a source to the Part II of Hochschild, cyclotomic and refined trace methods proposed by the AMMN extraction (RefinedTraceMethodsPartIIPadicDeformationOfKTheoryClasses). That brief already requires 'continuous invariants F^cts(X) = lim_n F(X_n) … with the continuity theorems saying which comparison maps F(X) → F^cts(X) are p-adic equivalences'. Clausen–Mathew–Morrow §5.2 proves those theorems for K and TC.

Plan:
- nilpotent, quickly converging, almost nilpotent and almost quickly converging towers of abelian groups and spectra, with Mathew's Lemmas 3.10–3.11 (Geom. Topol. 19) and Lemmas 5.13, 5.15–5.18;
- Theorem 5.19 (continuity of THH and TC along almost quickly converging towers of connective E_1-rings);
- Theorem 5.20 (derived p-adic continuity of TC for HZ-algebras);
- Theorem 5.21 (derived p-adic continuity of K when π_0 is commutative and henselian along p);
- Theorem 5.22 (p-adic continuity of K for commutative rings henselian along p with bounded p-power torsion, generalizing Geisser–Hesselholt's Theorem 5.10);
- Remark 5.23's variant over the sphere.

Import Theorem 4.36 (rigidity for henselian pairs) and Remark 2.8 from the proposed RefinedTraceMethodsPartIIHenselianPairs, and TC and THH from RefinedTraceMethods RT.2.

Unit tests:
- R = Z_p;
- R = Z_p[x]/(px), with bounded p-torsion;
- a ring with unbounded p-power torsion, where Theorem 5.22 does not apply.
